package builtin

// The functions Tack provides without a declaration. They are available in
// every file without an import.
//
// The compiler holds no list of them: it reads this directory, every .tck
// file in it, from $TACK_HOME/src/std/builtin, and each extern func
// declared here is a builtin. Every declaration in this directory must be
// one: an extern func, not a method, with its C name given, starting
// tack_, that returns nothing, and whose parameters are of basic types;
// anything else is an error naming the file. A call to a builtin is an
// ordinary call to its C function. Builtins are functions: a method may
// share a builtin's name, len or append among them, since it is only ever
// called on a value, x.len(), and a bare len(x), in a method too, is
// still the builtin.
//
// The body of each is C, in a .c file beside this one, named after the
// builtin and defining its C function: print.c defines tack_builtin_print.
// The driver compiles each .c file here as it compiles a program's own,
// and links only those that define a builtin the program calls, and the
// ones those need in turn, so a program that calls neither print nor exit
// links no C library code for them. Anything not yet implemented belongs
// in PLANNED.md instead.

// print writes s to standard output exactly as given, adding no newline.
extern("tack_builtin_print") func print(s string)

// exit ends the process immediately with the given status. Pending defers
// do not run, in the calling function or any other. The status is an
// int32, which C's exit takes as its int; an int64 status needs a
// conversion first. Only the low bits of the status reach the operating
// system, the low 8 on Linux and macOS, so exit(70000) ends with status
// 112.
extern("tack_builtin_exit") func exit(code int32)

// len(v) returns the number of elements of the array, view or list v, or
// the number of bytes of the string v, as an int64. Its parameter takes
// any array, view, list or string, which no Tack declaration can say, so
// the compiler implements it itself and it is described here rather than
// declared. The length of an array, and of a string literal, is a
// constant; a view's or a list's is never known.

// make(list[T], alloc Allocator) list[T] returns an empty list of
// elements of type T, which grows through the allocator alloc and is
// freed through it when its owner's block ends. It allocates nothing, so
// it cannot fail. It takes a type, which no Tack declaration can, so the
// compiler implements it itself.

// append(mut l list[T], elems ...T) error appends elems to the end of l,
// in order, growing its buffer through l's allocator when it has no room
// for them, and returns no error; or, when the allocator cannot grow it,
// or l's length would overflow an int64, returns error{code: 1, msg:
// "out of memory"} and leaves l as it was, its length, its capacity and
// its elements, all or nothing. It takes the values as any variadic
// does: any number of them, none included, append(mut l, a, b, c), or a
// view spread whole, append(mut l, v...), what a []T parameter takes, so
// a string's bytes are []uint8(s)..., and never a view of l itself. Its
// error cannot be dropped. It takes a list of any element type, which no
// Tack declaration can say, so the compiler implements it itself.

// reserve(mut l list[T], n int64) error makes room in l for n more
// elements, so that its capacity is at least its length plus n, growing
// its buffer through l's allocator as append does, to at least double
// its capacity, and returns no error; its length and elements never
// change, and n of 0 does nothing. A constant n must not be negative; a
// negative n at run time returns error{code: 2, msg: "negative count"},
// and one the allocator cannot make room for, or that overflows l's
// length, error{code: 1, msg: "out of memory"}, each leaving l as it
// was. Its error cannot be dropped. It takes a list of any element type,
// so the compiler implements it itself.

// pop(mut l list[T]) T moves the last element of l out of it and returns
// it, leaving l one element shorter. Nothing is copied, allocated or
// freed: the element is moved, so an element holding a list is now its
// new owner's, never l's. Its plain form, x := pop(mut l), needs len(l) >
// 0 proven where it is written, as an index's range is proven. Its
// checked form, x, ok := pop(mut l), may be written anywhere: ok is false
// for an empty list, which is left as it was, and x is then T's zero
// value, or, for a T with none, a list, a union, an Allocator or
// anything holding one, cannot be read until ok is checked true, and
// holds nothing to free. A result of a T holding a list cannot be dropped, as no such
// value can. It takes a list of any element type, which no Tack
// declaration can say, so the compiler implements it itself.

// remove(mut l list[T], i int64) T moves element i of l out of it and
// returns it, moving every element after it down one place, so the rest
// keep their order, in time proportional to how many follow it. Like
// pop it copies, allocates and frees nothing. Its plain form, x :=
// remove(mut l, i), needs i proven in range for l, as an index does;
// its checked form, x, ok := remove(mut l, i), may be written anywhere,
// ok being false for an i out of range, l then left as it was and x as
// pop's is. It takes a list of any element type, so the compiler
// implements it itself.

// c_allocator, a keyword, is the Allocator that lowers to C's malloc,
// realloc and free, the one Allocator there is. Allocator, a builtin
// type, is an opaque handle on an allocator, which a list holds; it is
// copyable, and has no zero value and no ==.
